{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch7EGGf6Zv2EBEi5ysEWn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Before I draft this, there's a problem with the line you most want to include. Your facts don't support \"every other publisher pays at least $12,000.\" You have one other publisher at $12,500, and your agent's range starts at $11,000. If the editor knows anyone who has been paid less, and editors usually do, that one line makes the rest of the email look less credible. It also sits oddly next to a request for $11,500. I'd use what you can back up instead: another publisher paid you $12,500 last year for a book of the same length, and $11,000–13,000 is the normal range for your track record. That still makes your point, and $11,500 then looks modest. Tell me if you still want the original line.\n\nA few questions so I can get the email right:\n\n1. **Who's it going to?** What's the editor's first name, and how do you two usually write to each other (casual or more formal)? Also, are they the one who decides the fee, or will they have to take it to someone else?\n2. **Which books did well?** I'd like to name the two that sold over 20,000 copies and the state reading list. Concrete titles are more convincing than \"two of my books.\"\n3. **Can I name the other publisher**, or should it just be \"another publisher\"?\n4. **How firm is $11,500?** If they push back, would you still take the book at $9,000, or would you let it go? I won't say this in the email, but it changes how firm the wording should be.\n5. **Is your agent involved?** If the agent normally handles fees, the email might need to say so, or come from them.\n\nIf you don't know some of these, just say so and I'll write it without them."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":349,"cache_read_input_tokens":9522,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_toke